WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.030
Welcome to Week Nine of CS104.

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:09.090
This lecture is about mathematical induction: a way to prove a statement

3
00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:15.150
for every integer in a specified range without checking the integers one

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.170
at a time forever.

5
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:23.230
We will learn how a base case and an inductive step work

6
00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:29.290
together, apply the method to sums, divisibility, and inequalities, and diagnose two

7
00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.310
convincing but faulty proofs.

8
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:37.370
Then we will use strong induction and multiple base cases when the

9
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.915
next result depends on more than one earlier result.

10
00:00:41.915 --> 00:00:47.975
Throughout, pay attention to exactly where the statement starts and what the

11
00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:50.500
induction hypothesis actually gives us.

12
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.376
Here is the route through the lecture.

13
00:00:54.376 --> 00:01:01.022
Section seven point one introduces the ordinary principle of mathematical induction and

14
00:01:01.022 --> 00:01:04.344
then tests our understanding with fallacies.

15
00:01:04.344 --> 00:01:10.989
A calculation in the inductive step can look impeccable while the overall

16
00:01:10.989 --> 00:01:12.651
proof still fails.

17
00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:16.527
Section seven point two develops other variants.

18
00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:23.172
Strong induction lets us assume all earlier cases when proving the next

19
00:01:23.172 --> 00:01:23.726
one.

20
00:01:23.726 --> 00:01:30.371
Multiple base cases handle statements that depend on several preceding terms, especially

21
00:01:30.371 --> 00:01:31.478
recursive sequences.

22
00:01:31.478 --> 00:01:38.124
These methods share the same logic: establish a starting foothold, then show

23
00:01:38.124 --> 00:01:42.000
that the property cannot stop being true.

24
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.299
We start with the ordinary induction principle.

25
00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:49.540
Suppose a statement depends on a positive integer n.

26
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:55.195
A few successful examples do not prove it for every positive integer,

27
00:01:55.195 --> 00:01:59.908
because there is always another value we have not checked.

28
00:01:59.908 --> 00:02:05.563
Induction replaces that endless list with two finite tasks: verify the first

29
00:02:05.563 --> 00:02:11.218
value, and prove that any true case leads to the next one.

30
00:02:11.218 --> 00:02:13.103
The word any matters.

31
00:02:13.103 --> 00:02:18.759
We prove the step for an arbitrary integer in the range, not

32
00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:23.000
just for the first few values on the slide.

33
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.520
Imagine a ladder with any number of steps.

34
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.160
First, we can reach step one.

35
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.440
Second, whenever we stand on step k, we can reach step k

36
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.320
plus one.

37
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.600
For a four-step ladder, start at one and apply the second rule

38
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.560
successively for k equal to one, two, and three.

39
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.840
The same pair of rules works no matter how tall the ladder

40
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:50.280
is.

41
00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.560
The first rule is the base case; the second is the inductive

42
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.000
step.

43
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.760
Neither rule alone suffices.

44
00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.040
A way to move upward is useless without a starting step, while

45
00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.000
reaching step one tells us nothing about higher steps.

46
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.652
The theorem makes the ladder precise.

47
00:03:09.652 --> 00:03:14.071
Let p of n be a statement for positive integers.

48
00:03:14.071 --> 00:03:15.839
Prove p of one.

49
00:03:15.839 --> 00:03:21.143
Then take an arbitrary positive integer k, assume p of k, and

50
00:03:21.143 --> 00:03:25.121
under that assumption prove p of k plus one.

51
00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:30.424
We may then conclude that p of n holds for every positive

52
00:03:30.424 --> 00:03:31.308
integer n.

53
00:03:31.308 --> 00:03:35.728
The assumption p of k is called the induction hypothesis.

54
00:03:35.728 --> 00:03:41.031
We do not assume the entire claim we are trying to prove;

55
00:03:41.031 --> 00:03:46.335
we use one conditional step to connect each case to the next.

56
00:03:46.335 --> 00:03:51.638
If the intended range begins at a number other than one, move

57
00:03:51.638 --> 00:03:56.500
the base case and the step range to that starting number.

58
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.317
Try induction on two familiar finite sums.

59
00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:04.146
The first says one plus two through n equals the product of

60
00:04:04.146 --> 00:04:08.573
n and n plus one, divided by two, for positive n.

61
00:04:08.573 --> 00:04:13.402
The second says one plus r through r to the power n

62
00:04:13.402 --> 00:04:18.232
equals the quantity r raised to the exponent n plus one, minus

63
00:04:18.232 --> 00:04:23.061
one, all divided by r minus one, for integers n at least

64
00:04:23.061 --> 00:04:23.463
zero.

65
00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:27.085
That displayed fraction requires r not equal to one.

66
00:04:27.085 --> 00:04:31.915
If r equals one, there are n plus one terms, each equal

67
00:04:31.915 --> 00:04:35.939
to one, so the sum is simply n plus one.

68
00:04:35.939 --> 00:04:40.768
Notice also that the geometric sum starts at n equal to zero,

69
00:04:40.768 --> 00:04:45.598
so its base case is p of zero rather than p of

70
00:04:45.598 --> 00:04:46.000
one.

71
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.811
For the arithmetic sum, the base case n equals one reads one

72
00:04:50.811 --> 00:04:53.617
equals one times two divided by two.

73
00:04:53.617 --> 00:04:58.428
Now assume that the sum through k is the product of k

74
00:04:58.428 --> 00:05:01.234
and k plus one, divided by two.

75
00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:06.045
To obtain the sum through k plus one, append the new term

76
00:05:06.045 --> 00:05:07.248
k plus one.

77
00:05:07.248 --> 00:05:09.252
Factor out k plus one.

78
00:05:09.252 --> 00:05:14.063
The other factor is k divided by two, plus one, which equals

79
00:05:14.063 --> 00:05:16.468
k plus two divided by two.

80
00:05:16.468 --> 00:05:21.279
So the result is the product of k plus one and k

81
00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:23.284
plus two, divided by two.

82
00:05:23.284 --> 00:05:28.095
That is exactly the formula with n replaced by k plus one.

83
00:05:28.095 --> 00:05:30.500
We have proved both required parts.

84
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:35.438
For the geometric sum, assume r is not one and define p

85
00:05:35.438 --> 00:05:38.319
of n for n at least zero.

86
00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:43.258
At n equals zero, the left side is one, and the formula

87
00:05:43.258 --> 00:05:47.373
gives r minus one over r minus one, also one.

88
00:05:47.373 --> 00:05:52.312
Assume the sum through r to the power k equals the quantity

89
00:05:52.312 --> 00:05:57.250
r raised to the exponent k plus one, minus one, all divided

90
00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:58.896
by r minus one.

91
00:05:58.896 --> 00:06:03.835
Add the next term, r raised to the exponent k plus one.

92
00:06:03.835 --> 00:06:08.773
After putting both terms over the common denominator r minus one, the

93
00:06:08.773 --> 00:06:13.712
numerator simplifies to r raised to the exponent k plus two, minus

94
00:06:13.712 --> 00:06:14.123
one.

95
00:06:14.123 --> 00:06:17.827
This is the required formula at k plus one.

96
00:06:17.827 --> 00:06:22.765
The case r equals one remains true by the separate direct expression,

97
00:06:22.765 --> 00:06:24.000
n plus one.

98
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.287
Our next proposition sums squares: one squared plus two squared through n

99
00:06:29.287 --> 00:06:34.574
squared equals the product of n, n plus one, and two n

100
00:06:34.574 --> 00:06:37.218
plus one, all divided by six.

101
00:06:37.218 --> 00:06:42.505
It may be hard to guess this formula, but once given, induction

102
00:06:42.505 --> 00:06:43.827
checks it systematically.

103
00:06:43.827 --> 00:06:46.030
Start at n equals one.

104
00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:51.317
The left side has the single term one squared, which is one.

105
00:06:51.317 --> 00:06:56.604
The right side becomes one times two times three divided by six,

106
00:06:56.604 --> 00:06:57.485
again one.

107
00:06:57.485 --> 00:06:59.688
That establishes the first case.

108
00:06:59.688 --> 00:07:04.975
The real work is now to show that the formula for k

109
00:07:04.975 --> 00:07:08.500
leads to the formula for k plus one.

110
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:13.711
The slide puts the induction hypothesis and the target side by side.

111
00:07:13.711 --> 00:07:18.921
Assume the sum of the first k squares is the product of

112
00:07:18.921 --> 00:07:24.132
k, k plus one, and two k plus one, divided by six.

113
00:07:24.132 --> 00:07:29.342
The target contains k plus one terms and should equal the product

114
00:07:29.342 --> 00:07:34.553
of k plus one, k plus two, and two k plus three,

115
00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:35.855
divided by six.

116
00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:41.066
The bridge is the one new term, the square of k plus

117
00:07:41.066 --> 00:07:41.500
one.

118
00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:46.711
Write the sum through k plus one as the known sum through

119
00:07:46.711 --> 00:07:48.447
k plus that square.

120
00:07:48.447 --> 00:07:52.789
This identifies precisely where we may substitute the induction hypothesis.

121
00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:58.000
We still have to simplify the resulting expression to match the target.

122
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.042
Substitute the induction hypothesis into that bridge.

123
00:08:01.042 --> 00:08:06.257
We have k times k plus one times two k plus one

124
00:08:06.257 --> 00:08:09.299
over six, plus k plus one squared.

125
00:08:09.299 --> 00:08:14.514
Factor out k plus one and use a common denominator of six.

126
00:08:14.514 --> 00:08:19.729
The remaining numerator is k times two k plus one, plus six

127
00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:21.467
times k plus one.

128
00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:25.813
Expanding gives two k squared plus seven k plus six.

129
00:08:25.813 --> 00:08:30.593
That factors as k plus two times two k plus three.

130
00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:35.808
Therefore the whole expression is k plus one times k plus two

131
00:08:35.808 --> 00:08:41.023
times two k plus three over six, exactly the formula at k

132
00:08:41.023 --> 00:08:41.893
plus one.

133
00:08:41.893 --> 00:08:44.500
This factorisation completes the inductive step.

134
00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.890
Induction also proves divisibility statements.

135
00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:52.626
The proposition says that, for every positive integer n, two to the

136
00:08:52.626 --> 00:08:56.929
power two n minus one is divisible by three.

137
00:08:56.929 --> 00:09:02.665
We will use the definition: an integer is divisible by three if

138
00:09:02.665 --> 00:09:05.533
it equals three times another integer.

139
00:09:05.533 --> 00:09:11.269
For the base case n equals one, two squared minus one equals

140
00:09:11.269 --> 00:09:11.747
three.

141
00:09:11.747 --> 00:09:16.049
Thus the required multiple of three is visible immediately.

142
00:09:16.049 --> 00:09:21.786
In the step, the challenge is to relate the exponent two k

143
00:09:21.786 --> 00:09:27.522
plus two to the exponent two k that appears in the induction

144
00:09:27.522 --> 00:09:28.000
hypothesis.

145
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.922
Make the hypothesis concrete.

146
00:09:29.922 --> 00:09:35.686
Assume that two raised to the exponent two k, minus one, equals

147
00:09:35.686 --> 00:09:38.569
three m for some integer m.

148
00:09:38.569 --> 00:09:44.333
Our goal is to find an integer s such that two raised

149
00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:50.098
to the exponent two times the quantity k plus one, minus one,

150
00:09:50.098 --> 00:09:51.539
equals three s.

151
00:09:51.539 --> 00:09:57.304
Naming the integer multiplier prevents us from treating divisibility as a vague

152
00:09:57.304 --> 00:09:57.784
claim.

153
00:09:57.784 --> 00:10:03.549
Since raising the exponent by two multiplies the power by four, we

154
00:10:03.549 --> 00:10:06.912
should rewrite four as three plus one.

155
00:10:06.912 --> 00:10:12.676
That will leave a copy of two raised to the exponent two

156
00:10:12.676 --> 00:10:17.000
k, minus one, exactly the expression the hypothesis controls.

157
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.838
Here is the calculation.

158
00:10:18.838 --> 00:10:24.354
The expression for k plus one equals four times two raised to

159
00:10:24.354 --> 00:10:27.111
the exponent two k, minus one.

160
00:10:27.111 --> 00:10:29.869
Replace four by three plus one.

161
00:10:29.869 --> 00:10:35.384
The result is three times two raised to two k, plus the

162
00:10:35.384 --> 00:10:39.061
quantity two raised to two k minus one.

163
00:10:39.061 --> 00:10:43.657
By the induction hypothesis, that last quantity is three m.

164
00:10:43.657 --> 00:10:49.172
Hence the whole expression equals three times the integer given by two

165
00:10:49.172 --> 00:10:51.929
raised to two k plus m.

166
00:10:51.929 --> 00:10:56.985
This supplies the required multiplier s and proves the next case.

167
00:10:56.985 --> 00:11:02.500
Together with the base case, it establishes divisibility for every positive n.

168
00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:07.395
The next practice problem is an inequality: prove that two n plus

169
00:11:07.395 --> 00:11:12.289
one is less than two to the power n for every integer

170
00:11:12.289 --> 00:11:13.921
n at least three.

171
00:11:13.921 --> 00:11:16.776
The threshold is part of the claim.

172
00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:21.671
At n equal to two, both sides are five and four respectively,

173
00:11:21.671 --> 00:11:25.342
so the inequality is false; starting at three matters.

174
00:11:25.342 --> 00:11:29.013
Check n equals three: seven is less than eight.

175
00:11:29.013 --> 00:11:33.908
For the step, add two to the left side to move from

176
00:11:33.908 --> 00:11:38.803
two k plus one to two times k plus one, plus one.

177
00:11:38.803 --> 00:11:43.697
On the right, moving from two to the k to two to

178
00:11:43.697 --> 00:11:48.592
the k plus one doubles the quantity, leaving room for the added

179
00:11:48.592 --> 00:11:49.000
two.

180
00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:53.696
The proof begins at n equals three, where seven is less than

181
00:11:53.696 --> 00:11:54.087
eight.

182
00:11:54.087 --> 00:11:58.783
Now take k at least three and assume two k plus one

183
00:11:58.783 --> 00:12:01.913
is less than two to the power k.

184
00:12:01.913 --> 00:12:06.609
The next left side is two k plus three, which is two

185
00:12:06.609 --> 00:12:08.957
more than the current left side.

186
00:12:08.957 --> 00:12:13.652
Therefore two k plus three is less than two to the power

187
00:12:13.652 --> 00:12:14.826
k plus two.

188
00:12:14.826 --> 00:12:19.522
Since k is at least three, two is less than two to

189
00:12:19.522 --> 00:12:20.696
the power k.

190
00:12:20.696 --> 00:12:25.391
So this is less than two to the power k plus two

191
00:12:25.391 --> 00:12:30.087
to the power k, or two to the power k plus one.

192
00:12:30.087 --> 00:12:34.000
We have reached the next case with strict inequalities throughout.

193
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.568
This slide deliberately offers a false formula: one plus two through n

194
00:12:39.568 --> 00:12:44.671
supposedly equals n squared plus n plus one, divided by two.

195
00:12:44.671 --> 00:12:46.991
Its inductive calculation works conditionally.

196
00:12:46.991 --> 00:12:52.559
If the false formula held at k, adding k plus one would

197
00:12:52.559 --> 00:12:56.734
produce the same false formula at k plus one.

198
00:12:56.734 --> 00:12:59.982
But there is no true starting case.

199
00:12:59.982 --> 00:13:05.550
At n equals one, the left side is one while the proposed

200
00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:07.869
right side is three halves.

201
00:13:07.869 --> 00:13:13.437
An implication from a false hypothesis can still be valid; it does

202
00:13:13.437 --> 00:13:16.685
not establish that the hypothesis ever holds.

203
00:13:16.685 --> 00:13:22.252
This is why we must verify the base case separately, even when

204
00:13:22.252 --> 00:13:25.500
the algebra in the step looks flawless.

205
00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:29.036
The famous cows argument has the opposite problem.

206
00:13:29.036 --> 00:13:34.339
A one-cow group certainly has one colour, so the base case is

207
00:13:34.339 --> 00:13:34.781
true.

208
00:13:34.781 --> 00:13:40.085
For a group of k plus one cows, the argument looks at

209
00:13:40.085 --> 00:13:45.388
the first k and last k cows, then says they share cows

210
00:13:45.388 --> 00:13:49.808
in the middle and must therefore have the same colour.

211
00:13:49.808 --> 00:13:54.228
That overlap exists only when k is at least two.

212
00:13:54.228 --> 00:13:59.531
At the crucial first step, k equals one, the two one-cow groups

213
00:13:59.531 --> 00:14:00.415
are disjoint.

214
00:14:00.415 --> 00:14:05.719
Nothing connects their colours, so p of one does not imply p

215
00:14:05.719 --> 00:14:06.603
of two.

216
00:14:06.603 --> 00:14:08.813
The induction chain breaks immediately.

217
00:14:08.813 --> 00:14:14.116
When checking a step, test it at the first value where it

218
00:14:14.116 --> 00:14:15.000
must operate.

219
00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:19.093
Section seven point two introduces variants of induction.

220
00:14:19.093 --> 00:14:24.721
The central question is how much earlier information a step needs.

221
00:14:24.721 --> 00:14:30.860
Ordinary induction presents p of k when proving p of k plus

222
00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:31.372
one.

223
00:14:31.372 --> 00:14:37.512
Strong induction presents all the statements from the base case through p

224
00:14:37.512 --> 00:14:38.535
of k.

225
00:14:38.535 --> 00:14:44.674
Later, if a recurrence uses two or three preceding terms, we will

226
00:14:44.674 --> 00:14:49.279
give the argument the appropriate number of starting cases.

227
00:14:49.279 --> 00:14:54.395
These are different proof formats for making the dependency explicit.

228
00:14:54.395 --> 00:14:59.000
We begin with the binary representation of positive integers.

229
00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:04.543
Every positive integer can be written as a sum of distinct powers

230
00:15:04.543 --> 00:15:05.467
of two.

231
00:15:05.467 --> 00:15:07.315
Four is two squared.

232
00:15:07.315 --> 00:15:11.011
Eleven is two cubed plus two plus one.

233
00:15:11.011 --> 00:15:15.168
Fifty-four is thirty-two plus sixteen plus four plus two.

234
00:15:15.168 --> 00:15:20.712
Distinct means no power appears twice in the sum; each power is

235
00:15:20.712 --> 00:15:24.870
either present or absent, just like a binary digit.

236
00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:29.951
The base case is one equals two to the power zero.

237
00:15:29.951 --> 00:15:35.495
To prove this for all positive integers, we need a reliable way

238
00:15:35.495 --> 00:15:41.038
to construct a representation for the next integer while keeping the powers

239
00:15:41.038 --> 00:15:41.500
distinct.

240
00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:44.413
A first attempt uses ordinary induction.

241
00:15:44.413 --> 00:15:50.240
Assume k is already a sum of distinct powers of two and

242
00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:54.611
add one, which is two to the power zero.

243
00:15:54.611 --> 00:16:00.438
That does not automatically give distinct powers: if the representation of k

244
00:16:00.438 --> 00:16:04.322
already contains one, the power zero appears twice.

245
00:16:04.322 --> 00:16:10.149
We could develop a carrying argument, as in binary arithmetic, but the

246
00:16:10.149 --> 00:16:15.490
simple line on the slide does not prove the next case.

247
00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:21.317
This is a gap in that attempted proof, not a failure of

248
00:16:21.317 --> 00:16:24.231
ordinary induction as a logical principle.

249
00:16:24.231 --> 00:16:30.058
The lecture now chooses strong induction, which gives a particularly clean argument

250
00:16:30.058 --> 00:16:32.000
using a smaller remainder.

251
00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:35.995
Strong induction begins with p of one as before.

252
00:16:35.995 --> 00:16:41.321
For an arbitrary k, assume every case p of one through p

253
00:16:41.321 --> 00:16:46.648
of k is true, and use all of them as needed to

254
00:16:46.648 --> 00:16:49.311
prove p of k plus one.

255
00:16:49.311 --> 00:16:54.638
The diagram shows the growing stock of established cases feeding each next

256
00:16:54.638 --> 00:16:55.082
case.

257
00:16:55.082 --> 00:17:00.408
This is a valid induction principle, not permission to assume the conclusion.

258
00:17:00.408 --> 00:17:05.735
The hypotheses stop at k; the claim to be proved is k

259
00:17:05.735 --> 00:17:06.622
plus one.

260
00:17:06.622 --> 00:17:11.949
One can justify the principle by applying ordinary induction to the combined

261
00:17:11.949 --> 00:17:15.500
statement that all cases through k are true.

262
00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:21.184
For the binary-sum proposition, the strong induction hypothesis says that each positive

263
00:17:21.184 --> 00:17:26.868
integer from one through k has a representation using distinct powers of

264
00:17:26.868 --> 00:17:27.342
two.

265
00:17:27.342 --> 00:17:30.184
We must represent k plus one.

266
00:17:30.184 --> 00:17:35.868
A useful move is to choose the largest power of two no

267
00:17:35.868 --> 00:17:38.237
greater than k plus one.

268
00:17:38.237 --> 00:17:43.447
If k plus one is exactly that power, we are done.

269
00:17:43.447 --> 00:17:44.868
Otherwise, subtract it.

270
00:17:44.868 --> 00:17:50.553
The remainder is positive and smaller than k plus one, so it

271
00:17:50.553 --> 00:17:52.921
falls within the strong hypothesis.

272
00:17:52.921 --> 00:17:58.605
We then need to check that the remainder's powers cannot repeat the

273
00:17:58.605 --> 00:18:00.500
large power we selected.

274
00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:06.104
Let two to the power j be the largest power of two

275
00:18:06.104 --> 00:18:08.906
no greater than k plus one.

276
00:18:08.906 --> 00:18:13.108
If equality holds, that single power is the representation.

277
00:18:13.108 --> 00:18:18.712
Otherwise write k plus one as two to the j plus a

278
00:18:18.712 --> 00:18:20.113
positive remainder m.

279
00:18:20.113 --> 00:18:25.717
Maximality of j implies m is less than two to the j,

280
00:18:25.717 --> 00:18:28.986
and certainly m is at most k.

281
00:18:28.986 --> 00:18:33.656
The strong hypothesis represents m using distinct powers of two.

282
00:18:33.656 --> 00:18:39.259
Every power in that representation has exponent below j, because m itself

283
00:18:39.259 --> 00:18:42.528
is smaller than two to the j.

284
00:18:42.528 --> 00:18:46.264
Adding the selected power therefore creates no duplicate.

285
00:18:46.264 --> 00:18:50.000
Both cases are covered, completing the induction proof.

286
00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:55.466
This slide uses strong induction in a different setting: every rational number

287
00:18:55.466 --> 00:18:58.654
can be represented by a continued fraction.

288
00:18:58.654 --> 00:19:04.120
Under the recursive description here, an integer is a continued fraction, and

289
00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:09.586
we can form a new one by taking an integer q plus

290
00:19:09.586 --> 00:19:12.318
one over a nonzero continued fraction.

291
00:19:12.318 --> 00:19:15.962
Consider a rational number with positive denominator d.

292
00:19:15.962 --> 00:19:20.062
At d equal to one it is an integer.

293
00:19:20.062 --> 00:19:25.527
For larger d, use division with remainder to write its numerator as

294
00:19:25.527 --> 00:19:30.993
q d plus r, where zero is at most r and r

295
00:19:30.993 --> 00:19:32.815
is less than d.

296
00:19:32.815 --> 00:19:36.459
If r is zero, the number is q.

297
00:19:36.459 --> 00:19:41.925
Otherwise it equals q plus one divided by the fraction d over

298
00:19:41.925 --> 00:19:42.380
r.

299
00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:47.846
The inner rational number, d over r, has positive denominator r, smaller

300
00:19:47.846 --> 00:19:51.945
than d, so the strong induction hypothesis covers it.

301
00:19:51.945 --> 00:19:56.500
This is the Euclidean algorithm expressed as a continued fraction.

302
00:19:56.500 --> 00:20:00.450
Now the step may depend on two adjacent earlier statements.

303
00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:05.190
The first variation says: prove p of one and p of two.

304
00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:09.930
Then, for every k at least two, assume both p of k

305
00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:14.670
minus one and p of k, and prove p of k plus

306
00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:15.065
one.

307
00:20:15.065 --> 00:20:19.805
At k equals two, the two base cases give p of three.

308
00:20:19.805 --> 00:20:24.545
At k equals three, p of two and the newly established p

309
00:20:24.545 --> 00:20:28.100
of three give p of four, and so on.

310
00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:32.840
One base case would be insufficient for this step, because it asks

311
00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:36.000
for two earlier facts at its first application.

312
00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:41.228
The general variation has d consecutive base cases: p of one through

313
00:20:41.228 --> 00:20:42.535
p of d.

314
00:20:42.535 --> 00:20:47.762
For each k at least d, a step may use the d

315
00:20:47.762 --> 00:20:52.990
most recent statements, from p of k minus d plus one through

316
00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:57.347
p of k, to prove p of k plus one.

317
00:20:57.347 --> 00:21:02.574
This describes the dependency pattern of a recurrence using d preceding terms.

318
00:21:02.574 --> 00:21:07.802
The bookkeeping is important: the first step begins at k equal to

319
00:21:07.802 --> 00:21:11.723
d and uses exactly the d statements already verified.

320
00:21:11.723 --> 00:21:16.950
Once the next statement is obtained, the window shifts forward one place.

321
00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:20.000
The next example uses three base cases.

322
00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:24.466
Define a sequence with first terms one, one, and three.

323
00:21:24.466 --> 00:21:28.932
Every later term is the sum of the preceding three.

324
00:21:28.932 --> 00:21:34.291
Thus the fourth, fifth, and sixth terms are five, nine, and seventeen,

325
00:21:34.291 --> 00:21:35.184
as shown.

326
00:21:35.184 --> 00:21:40.544
The proposition claims that a sub n is at most two to

327
00:21:40.544 --> 00:21:44.563
the power n minus one for every positive n.

328
00:21:44.563 --> 00:21:49.922
Check the three required starting cases: one is at most one, one

329
00:21:49.922 --> 00:21:54.388
is at most two, and three is at most four.

330
00:21:54.388 --> 00:21:59.748
To prove the next bound, we will need the bounds for all

331
00:21:59.748 --> 00:22:02.427
three terms appearing in the recurrence.

332
00:22:02.427 --> 00:22:06.000
This is why the multiple-base-case form fits naturally.

333
00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:10.655
Take k at least three and assume the required bounds for a

334
00:22:10.655 --> 00:22:15.310
sub k minus two, a sub k minus one, and a sub

335
00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:15.698
k.

336
00:22:15.698 --> 00:22:19.966
The recurrence gives a sub k plus one as their sum.

337
00:22:19.966 --> 00:22:24.621
The hypotheses bound that sum by two to the k minus three,

338
00:22:24.621 --> 00:22:29.276
plus two to the k minus two, plus two to the k

339
00:22:29.276 --> 00:22:30.052
minus one.

340
00:22:30.052 --> 00:22:34.707
Factor out two to the k minus three: the coefficient is one

341
00:22:34.707 --> 00:22:37.034
plus two plus four, or seven.

342
00:22:37.034 --> 00:22:41.690
Since seven is at most eight, the result is at most eight

343
00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:46.345
times two to the k minus three, which equals two to the

344
00:22:46.345 --> 00:22:46.733
k.

345
00:22:46.733 --> 00:22:51.000
That is precisely the target bound at index k plus one.

346
00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:55.404
The final practice problem starts a sequence at index zero.

347
00:22:55.404 --> 00:23:00.688
We are given s sub zero equals zero, s sub one equals

348
00:23:00.688 --> 00:23:05.972
four, and each later term equals six times the preceding term minus

349
00:23:05.972 --> 00:23:08.615
five times the term before that.

350
00:23:08.615 --> 00:23:13.899
The proposed closed form is s sub n equals five to the

351
00:23:13.899 --> 00:23:18.303
power n minus one for all n at least zero.

352
00:23:18.303 --> 00:23:23.587
Because the recurrence looks back two places, verify the formula at both

353
00:23:23.587 --> 00:23:24.908
zero and one.

354
00:23:24.908 --> 00:23:30.193
The inductive step must use both formulas at k minus one and

355
00:23:30.193 --> 00:23:34.156
k to prove the formula at k plus one.

356
00:23:34.156 --> 00:23:39.000
Keep those two hypotheses visible when you substitute into the recurrence.

357
00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:44.298
At zero, five to the power zero minus one is zero; at

358
00:23:44.298 --> 00:23:46.947
one, five minus one is four.

359
00:23:46.947 --> 00:23:49.596
Both bases match the given values.

360
00:23:49.596 --> 00:23:52.687
Now let k be at least one.

361
00:23:52.687 --> 00:23:57.985
The first induction hypothesis gives s sub k minus one as five

362
00:23:57.985 --> 00:24:02.401
raised to the exponent k minus one, then subtract one.

363
00:24:02.401 --> 00:24:07.699
The second gives s sub k as five to the power k,

364
00:24:07.699 --> 00:24:09.023
then subtract one.

365
00:24:09.023 --> 00:24:14.322
The recurrence says to multiply s sub k by six and subtract

366
00:24:14.322 --> 00:24:17.412
five times s sub k minus one.

367
00:24:17.412 --> 00:24:22.711
After substitution, the first contribution is six times the difference between five

368
00:24:22.711 --> 00:24:25.360
to the power k and one.

369
00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:30.658
The second is five times the difference between five raised to the

370
00:24:30.658 --> 00:24:33.307
exponent k minus one and one.

371
00:24:33.307 --> 00:24:36.398
Subtract the second contribution from the first.

372
00:24:36.398 --> 00:24:41.696
The power terms simplify to five to the power k plus one,

373
00:24:41.696 --> 00:24:44.345
while the constants give negative one.

374
00:24:44.345 --> 00:24:47.877
This proves the formula at k plus one.

375
00:24:47.877 --> 00:24:53.175
The step needed both preceding claims, even though the slide's closing shorthand

376
00:24:53.175 --> 00:24:54.500
names only one.
